## Required reading before performance-sensitive code

| Doc | Trigger — read it BEFORE writing or editing... |
|---|---|
| `DOCS/STRATEGY_AND_CODING_RULES.md` | any code in hot path, slow path, OMS drainer, parsing, ML inference, strategies, RollingStats / RegimeSignals / ExecutionCore — i.e., anything in `CoreFrameworks/`, `Strategies/`, `ML_Headers/`, `DataStream/`, or `FixedPoint/`. 11 strict invariants (no malloc, no virtual, no mutex, no sleep_for, no atof/strstr in parsers, cache alignment, branchless, AVX-512-friendly layouts, SCHED_FIFO/isolcpus deployment). |
| `DOCS/LATENCY_OPTIMIZATION_AUDIT.md` | any optimization investigation, performance-regression triage. 13 parts, ~40 specific items: hot path, slow path SIMD, arch invariants, ingestion+ML+memory, system tail variance, allocator eradication, parsing, OMS variance, concurrency, FPN libs, HFT system ops, hardware/OS tuning. |
| `plans/_cross-cutting/2026-05-06-latency-path-discipline.md` | any modification to **any code on a latency-impacting path**: hot path (`ExecutionCore_Tick`, `BG_Evaluate`, `SG_Evaluate`, `GateParameters`, `ParameterSlot`), slow path (`EventLoop_RebuildOneCore`, `RollingStats_Push`, `Regime_ComputeSignals`), OMS drainer (`OMS_DrainSubmit`, `OrderManager_Tick`), producer fan-out, snapshot publish, parsing. 7 architectural rules scaled by cadence + anti-pattern history + pre-merge verification checklist. |
| `tick-trader-percore-workspace/DOCS/DESIGN_PHILOSOPHY.md` | cold-pickup of an unfamiliar surface (which family of principles applies?), designing a non-trivial change (which discipline tier?), new contributor onboarding, making a cross-family trade-off, codifying a new pattern. 14 sections: cost framework + 13 hard invariants + 9 family sections (data-oriented design / latency / determinism / concurrency / structural-fix / failure observability / architectural primitives / operator UX / process discipline) + explicit NOs + cross-reference index. |

**Workflow:** open the rules doc first, scan relevant sections, then
write code. The audit doc is consulted when the rule is "yes but how"
— it has concrete per-site suggestions with file:line refs. The
hot-path discipline doc is the third leg — read when touching hot-path
layout / per-tick code. The philosophy doc is the fourth leg — read
when designing rather than implementing (which family of principles
governs this surface? what discipline tier am I choosing into?).

